1
00:00:00,250 --> 00:00:03,060
The phone in your pocket can do
billions of calculations

2
00:00:03,070 --> 00:00:04,430
every second.

3
00:00:05,250 --> 00:00:08,480
Eighty years ago, a machine that
could do just five

4
00:00:08,490 --> 00:00:11,340
thousand calculations a second
filled an entire

5
00:00:11,350 --> 00:00:12,150
room,

6
00:00:12,590 --> 00:00:16,040
weighed thirty tonnes, and drew
a hundred and fifty kilowatts

7
00:00:16,050 --> 00:00:16,850
of power.

8
00:00:17,333 --> 00:00:20,123
It was called ENIAC, and it was
one of the first

9
00:00:20,133 --> 00:00:22,313
general-purpose electronic
computers.

10
00:00:23,333 --> 00:00:26,803
This is the story of how it was
built, the women who programmed

11
00:00:26,813 --> 00:00:27,543
it,

12
00:00:27,553 --> 00:00:28,793
and how it changed the world.

13
00:00:30,400 --> 00:00:33,600
For centuries, a computer wasn't
a machine at all.

14
00:00:34,060 --> 00:00:37,210
It was a job title: a person who
did calculations

15
00:00:37,220 --> 00:00:38,340
by hand.

16
00:00:39,083 --> 00:00:42,013
In the eighteen twenties, the
English mathematician

17
00:00:42,023 --> 00:00:45,213
Charles Babbage designed a
mechanical calculator

18
00:00:45,223 --> 00:00:48,653
called the Difference Engine,
made of thousands of brass

19
00:00:48,663 --> 00:00:49,463
gears.

20
00:00:50,000 --> 00:00:51,680
Babbage never finished it.

21
00:00:52,160 --> 00:00:55,530
But in nineteen ninety one,
engineers at London's Science

22
00:00:55,540 --> 00:00:58,750
Museum built his second
Difference Engine from his
original

23
00:00:58,760 --> 00:00:59,560
drawings,

24
00:00:59,920 --> 00:01:00,860
and it worked perfectly.

25
00:01:01,750 --> 00:01:05,220
Later, he designed a far more
ambitious Analytical

26
00:01:05,230 --> 00:01:06,030
Engine,

27
00:01:06,530 --> 00:01:09,010
which could be programmed with
punched cards.

28
00:01:09,550 --> 00:01:12,700
Ada Lovelace wrote what's often
called the first

29
00:01:12,710 --> 00:01:14,510
computer program for it.

30
00:01:14,630 --> 00:01:15,870
But it was never built.

31
00:01:16,667 --> 00:01:19,997
A century later, war created an
urgent need for

32
00:01:20,007 --> 00:01:20,807
calculation.

33
00:01:21,500 --> 00:01:24,970
In Britain, codebreakers at
Bletchley Park built

34
00:01:24,980 --> 00:01:25,780
Colossus,

35
00:01:26,200 --> 00:01:29,020
an electronic machine that
helped crack German codes.

36
00:01:29,760 --> 00:01:31,260
It was kept secret for decades.

37
00:01:32,250 --> 00:01:35,540
Its designer, a Post Office
engineer named

38
00:01:35,550 --> 00:01:38,320
Tommy Flowers, used around two
and a half thousand

39
00:01:38,330 --> 00:01:41,780
vacuum tubes, and paid for some
of

40
00:01:41,790 --> 00:01:43,330
the parts out of his own pocket.

41
00:01:43,830 --> 00:01:46,390
By the end of the war, ten of
them were running.

42
00:01:47,167 --> 00:01:50,257
In Germany, Konrad Zuse had
already built the

43
00:01:50,267 --> 00:01:53,417
Z3 in nineteen forty one, a
programmable

44
00:01:53,427 --> 00:01:56,707
machine that used thousands of
clicking telephone relays.

45
00:01:57,500 --> 00:02:00,990
And in Iowa, John Atanasoff and
Clifford Berry

46
00:02:01,000 --> 00:02:04,240
built an electronic calculator
for solving equations.

47
00:02:05,380 --> 00:02:08,570
Who built the first computer
depends on exactly how you
define

48
00:02:08,580 --> 00:02:09,380
one.

49
00:02:10,817 --> 00:02:13,497
In America, the army had a
different problem:

50
00:02:14,017 --> 00:02:14,817
artillery.

51
00:02:15,250 --> 00:02:18,220
To aim a big gun, soldiers
needed firing

52
00:02:18,230 --> 00:02:19,030
tables:

53
00:02:19,330 --> 00:02:22,440
books of numbers telling them
the angle to use for every

54
00:02:22,450 --> 00:02:23,250
distance,

55
00:02:23,470 --> 00:02:24,710
wind and temperature.

56
00:02:25,500 --> 00:02:28,590
Each trajectory took a human
computer, with a desk

57
00:02:28,600 --> 00:02:29,110
calculator,

58
00:02:29,120 --> 00:02:32,120
about a day or more to work out.

59
00:02:32,580 --> 00:02:34,820
A single table needed thousands
of them.

60
00:02:35,500 --> 00:02:38,830
At the University of
Pennsylvania, dozens of women

61
00:02:38,840 --> 00:02:40,380
worked on these calculations.

62
00:02:41,160 --> 00:02:42,000
They couldn't keep up.

63
00:02:43,400 --> 00:02:46,580
A physicist named John Mauchly
had an idea:

64
00:02:47,200 --> 00:02:50,370
build a calculator that used
electronic vacuum tubes

65
00:02:50,380 --> 00:02:52,020
instead of moving parts.

66
00:02:52,917 --> 00:02:56,027
The project was based at the
university's Moore School

67
00:02:56,037 --> 00:02:59,207
of Electrical Engineering, under
the code name

68
00:02:59,217 --> 00:03:00,477
Project PX.

69
00:03:01,137 --> 00:03:03,657
It eventually cost about half a
million dollars.

70
00:03:04,500 --> 00:03:07,950
A vacuum tube is like a light
bulb that can act as a

71
00:03:07,960 --> 00:03:08,760
switch,

72
00:03:08,840 --> 00:03:12,340
turning a current on and off
thousands of times a second,

73
00:03:12,660 --> 00:03:14,020
with nothing moving at all.

74
00:03:14,833 --> 00:03:17,093
With a brilliant young engineer,
J.

75
00:03:17,553 --> 00:03:20,883
Presper Eckert, Mauchly
convinced the army to pay

76
00:03:20,893 --> 00:03:23,031
for it in nineteen forty three.

77
00:03:23,500 --> 00:03:25,400
Experts warned that it would
never work.

78
00:03:25,800 --> 00:03:29,130
With so many tubes, they said,
one would burn out

79
00:03:29,140 --> 00:03:30,080
every few seconds.

80
00:03:31,000 --> 00:03:33,970
Eckert solved it by running the
tubes well below their limits,

81
00:03:33,980 --> 00:03:35,540
and choosing them carefully.

82
00:03:36,080 --> 00:03:39,100
Failures fell to about one tube
every two days.

83
00:03:39,917 --> 00:03:43,367
The finished machine had almost
eighteen thousand vacuum

84
00:03:43,377 --> 00:03:44,177
tubes,

85
00:03:44,257 --> 00:03:47,447
seventy thousand resistors, and
around five million

86
00:03:47,457 --> 00:03:48,737
hand-soldered joints.

87
00:03:49,667 --> 00:03:50,467
Surprisingly,

88
00:03:51,267 --> 00:03:52,797
it didn't count in binary.

89
00:03:52,807 --> 00:03:56,037
ENIAC worked in ordinary decimal
numbers,

90
00:03:56,047 --> 00:03:58,927
using rings of ten tubes to
store each digit.

91
00:03:59,833 --> 00:04:03,263
Its panels, arranged in a giant
U, stretched about

92
00:04:03,273 --> 00:04:06,433
thirty metres, and the whole
machine weighed thirty tonnes.

93
00:04:08,233 --> 00:04:11,663
Six women were chosen from the
human computers to program

94
00:04:11,673 --> 00:04:12,473
it:

95
00:04:12,673 --> 00:04:14,873
Kay McNulty, Betty Jennings,

96
00:04:15,513 --> 00:04:16,313
Betty Snyder,

97
00:04:16,893 --> 00:04:17,753
Marlyn Wescoff,

98
00:04:18,813 --> 00:04:20,753
Frances Bilas, and Ruth
Lichterman.

99
00:04:21,667 --> 00:04:24,347
There were no programming
languages, no keyboards,

100
00:04:25,227 --> 00:04:26,147
and no manuals.

101
00:04:26,767 --> 00:04:28,697
They were given the machine's
wiring diagrams,

102
00:04:28,707 --> 00:04:30,607
and told to work it out.

103
00:04:31,333 --> 00:04:34,433
Programming ENIAC meant
physically rewiring it:

104
00:04:34,853 --> 00:04:38,163
plugging hundreds of cables and
setting thousands of switches

105
00:04:38,173 --> 00:04:38,993
by hand.

106
00:04:39,653 --> 00:04:42,113
A new program could take days to
set up.

107
00:04:42,833 --> 00:04:46,293
They also invented techniques
that programmers still use,

108
00:04:46,933 --> 00:04:49,103
like breaking a problem into
sub-routines,

109
00:04:49,113 --> 00:04:52,373
and hunting down bugs by
stepping through the machine.

110
00:04:53,250 --> 00:04:54,070
Over at Harvard,

111
00:04:54,710 --> 00:04:57,880
Grace Hopper's team later taped
a real moth into a

112
00:04:57,890 --> 00:05:00,090
logbook after it jammed a
computer:

113
00:05:00,730 --> 00:05:03,330
the first actual case of bug
being found.

114
00:05:05,067 --> 00:05:07,387
ENIAC was finished too late for
the war.

115
00:05:07,787 --> 00:05:11,177
But on the fifteenth of
February, nineteen forty

116
00:05:11,187 --> 00:05:11,937
six,

117
00:05:11,947 --> 00:05:13,307
it was unveiled to the press.

118
00:05:14,250 --> 00:05:15,050
For the demonstration,

119
00:05:15,790 --> 00:05:19,180
it calculated the path of a
shell that takes thirty seconds

120
00:05:19,190 --> 00:05:21,870
to reach its target, in just
twenty seconds.

121
00:05:22,470 --> 00:05:24,430
Faster than the shell itself
could fly.

122
00:05:25,250 --> 00:05:28,700
The engineers had fitted
ping-pong balls cut in half over

123
00:05:28,710 --> 00:05:29,830
the indicator lights,

124
00:05:30,490 --> 00:05:33,070
so the blinking would look more
dramatic for the cameras.

125
00:05:34,083 --> 00:05:37,203
Newspapers called it a giant
electronic brain.

126
00:05:37,683 --> 00:05:39,963
Yet at the celebration dinner
afterwards,

127
00:05:40,443 --> 00:05:42,463
the six programmers were not
invited.

128
00:05:43,417 --> 00:05:46,737
Their contribution was
overlooked for half a century,

129
00:05:47,157 --> 00:05:50,547
until historians and a new
generation of programmers

130
00:05:50,557 --> 00:05:52,777
brought their story back to
light.

131
00:05:54,400 --> 00:05:56,120
ENIAC had a big weakness.

132
00:05:56,940 --> 00:06:00,150
Its program lived in its wiring,
so changing the task

133
00:06:00,160 --> 00:06:02,020
meant rebuilding the machine.

134
00:06:02,917 --> 00:06:06,127
The team, joined by the
mathematician John von

135
00:06:06,137 --> 00:06:06,937
Neumann,

136
00:06:07,317 --> 00:06:10,767
came up with a better design:
store the program in the

137
00:06:10,777 --> 00:06:13,117
computer's memory, just like
data.

138
00:06:13,917 --> 00:06:17,087
Alan Turing in Britain had
imagined such a

139
00:06:17,097 --> 00:06:20,333
universal machine back in
nineteen thirty

140
00:06:20,343 --> 00:06:20,987
six.

141
00:06:20,997 --> 00:06:24,027
In nineteen forty eight, a small
machine in

142
00:06:24,037 --> 00:06:24,837
Manchester,

143
00:06:25,197 --> 00:06:28,217
nicknamed the Baby, ran the
first stored program.

144
00:06:29,000 --> 00:06:32,300
Almost every computer since has
been built on that idea.

145
00:06:33,167 --> 00:06:35,477
Eckert and Mauchly started their
own company,

146
00:06:35,487 --> 00:06:38,577
and built UNIVAC, one of the
first computers

147
00:06:38,587 --> 00:06:39,627
sold to businesses.

148
00:06:40,407 --> 00:06:43,803
The first one went to the US
Census Bureau in nineteen

149
00:06:43,813 --> 00:06:44,977
fifty one.

150
00:06:44,987 --> 00:06:47,797
In nineteen fifty two, it
correctly predicted

151
00:06:47,807 --> 00:06:50,717
Eisenhower's landslide in the US
election,

152
00:06:50,727 --> 00:06:52,087
live on television.

153
00:06:53,083 --> 00:06:55,743
Betty Holberton, one of the
original six,

154
00:06:56,183 --> 00:06:58,943
went on to help develop early
programming languages,

155
00:06:59,543 --> 00:07:02,793
including COBOL, which still
runs many banking

156
00:07:02,803 --> 00:07:03,843
systems today.

157
00:07:05,483 --> 00:07:07,823
Next, the machines began to
shrink.

158
00:07:08,383 --> 00:07:11,673
In nineteen forty seven,
scientists at Bell Labs

159
00:07:11,683 --> 00:07:13,023
invented the transistor,

160
00:07:13,863 --> 00:07:17,333
a tiny solid switch that could
replace the fragile vacuum

161
00:07:17,343 --> 00:07:18,143
tube.

162
00:07:18,333 --> 00:07:21,603
Its inventors, John Bardeen,
Walter Brattain and

163
00:07:21,613 --> 00:07:25,083
William Shockley, shared the
Nobel Prize in Physics

164
00:07:25,093 --> 00:07:27,110
in nineteen fifty six.

165
00:07:27,583 --> 00:07:30,733
In the late nineteen fifties,
Jack Kilby and

166
00:07:30,743 --> 00:07:33,853
Robert Noyce worked out how to
put many transistors

167
00:07:33,863 --> 00:07:36,083
onto a single chip of silicon:

168
00:07:36,903 --> 00:07:38,063
the integrated circuit.

169
00:07:38,663 --> 00:07:42,153
Noyce went on to co-found Intel
with Gordon Moore,

170
00:07:42,163 --> 00:07:44,763
and Kilby later won a Nobel
Prize.

171
00:07:45,750 --> 00:07:49,180
In nineteen sixty five, Gordon
Moore predicted that

172
00:07:49,190 --> 00:07:52,680
the number of transistors on a
chip would keep doubling

173
00:07:52,690 --> 00:07:54,610
roughly every year or two.

174
00:07:55,110 --> 00:07:55,910
For decades,

175
00:07:56,270 --> 00:07:57,070
he was right.

176
00:07:57,417 --> 00:08:00,627
In nineteen seventy one, Intel
squeezed an entire

177
00:08:00,637 --> 00:08:03,867
computer processor onto one
chip, the

178
00:08:03,877 --> 00:08:07,347
four zero zero four, with about
two thousand three hundred

179
00:08:07,357 --> 00:08:08,257
transistors.

180
00:08:09,167 --> 00:08:12,537
By the late nineteen seventies,
computers had shrunk enough

181
00:08:12,547 --> 00:08:14,407
to sit on a desk at home.

182
00:08:15,167 --> 00:08:18,217
A modern smartphone chip holds
billions of

183
00:08:18,227 --> 00:08:19,027
transistors,

184
00:08:19,527 --> 00:08:21,247
each smaller than most viruses.

185
00:08:23,233 --> 00:08:26,493
ENIAC itself went on to
calculate weather forecasts,

186
00:08:27,133 --> 00:08:30,563
the design of nuclear weapons,
and the behaviour of wind

187
00:08:30,573 --> 00:08:32,373
tunnels and cosmic rays.

188
00:08:33,250 --> 00:08:36,010
In nineteen forty seven, it was
taken apart,

189
00:08:36,430 --> 00:08:39,640
moved to the army's Aberdeen
Proving Ground in

190
00:08:39,650 --> 00:08:40,420
Maryland,

191
00:08:40,430 --> 00:08:41,230
and rebuilt.

192
00:08:41,610 --> 00:08:45,080
It ran there, almost around the
clock, for eight more

193
00:08:45,090 --> 00:08:45,890
years.

194
00:08:46,250 --> 00:08:49,300
And in nineteen seventy three, a
court ruled that

195
00:08:49,310 --> 00:08:50,910
ENIAC's patent was invalid,

196
00:08:51,790 --> 00:08:55,180
partly because Atanasoff had got
there first with key

197
00:08:55,190 --> 00:08:56,090
ideas.

198
00:08:57,000 --> 00:09:00,290
It was switched off for the last
time in October nineteen

199
00:09:00,300 --> 00:09:01,530
fifty five.

200
00:09:01,540 --> 00:09:04,060
Parts of it survive in museums
today.

201
00:09:04,833 --> 00:09:08,123
In about eighty years, we went
from a thirty tonne machine that

202
00:09:08,133 --> 00:09:11,543
filled a room, to computers in
our pockets millions of

203
00:09:11,553 --> 00:09:12,653
times more powerful.

204
00:09:14,400 --> 00:09:17,630
Every app, every search, every
video you watch,

205
00:09:17,640 --> 00:09:21,130
traces its family tree back to a
room full of glowing

206
00:09:21,140 --> 00:09:21,590
tubes,

207
00:09:21,600 --> 00:09:24,360
and six women with a wiring
diagram.
